High-grade disease (CIN2 or CIN3&#x0002b;) was present in 21&#x00025; (561 out of 2618) of abnormal entry smears. HR-HPV was detected in the abnormal entry smear in 82&#x00025; (257 out of 314) of prevalent CIN3&#x0002b; cases, with a slightly higher rate in younger women. A second spatula was taken before the diagnostic biopsy in 21 of the 57 prevalent CIN3&#x0002b; cases whose entry spatula was HR-HPV negative, and 13 (62&#x00025;) were HR-HPV positive. The prevalence of HR-HPV at entry among cytologically normal women in whom CIN3&#x0002b; was diagnosed at the next routine smear was 50&#x00025; overall (42 out of 84: <xref rid="tbl3" ref-type="table">Table 3</xref>, right-hand column). This prevalence was similar in smears taken less than 3 years before CIN3 diagnosis (51&#x00025;:20 out of 39) or 3 or more years earlier (49&#x00025;:22 out of 45).</p><p>Prevalence rates at the next smear for CIN3&#x0002b;, CIN2 and lesser abnormality in the combined prospective and retrospective cohorts are shown in <xref rid="tbl4" ref-type="table">Table 4</xref> <table-wrap id="tbl4" position="float"><label>Table 4</label><caption><p content-type="table-title">Prevalence of CIN3&#x0002b;, CIN2 and lesser abnormality by interval since last normal smear and age</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" border="1" width="85%"><colgroup><col align="left"/><col align="char" char="."/><col align="left"/><col align="char" char="."/><col align="left"/><col align="char" char="."/><col align="left"/><col align="char" char="."/></colgroup><thead valign="bottom"><tr><th align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">&#x000a0;</th><th colspan="2" align="center" valign="top" char="." charoff="50"><bold>CIN3&#x0002b;</bold><hr/></th><th colspan="2" align="center" valign="top" char="." charoff="50"><bold>CIN2</bold><hr/></th><th colspan="2" align="center" valign="top" char="." charoff="50"><bold>Lesser abnormality</bold><hr/></th><th align="char" valign="top" char="." charoff="50">&#x000a0;</